RISC-V zkVM: zero GC-ref stack slots in the prolog only where a GC-free local would be zeroed (JitZkNoGcMustInit)

A zkVM guest links a GC that never collects (the Nethermind ZisK guest's uGC: GarbageCollect is a stub), so
no stack is ever scanned. The JIT still zeroes, in every prolog, each frame slot a GC could report before it is
written. On the Nethermind ZisK guest that is about three quarters of all executed DMA memsets (e.g. 152 bytes
on every ExecuteSLoad), plus the explicit zero stores and spill-temp stores that come with it.

JitZkNoGcMustInit=1 (default 0 = unchanged) gives a local or temp that holds GC references the zero-init
treatment of a GC-free local of the same shape. Disabled reasons, each one exists only for GC reporting:
- an untracked local with GC references is must-init (genCheckUseBlockInit): untracked GC slots are reported
  live for the whole method;
- a tracked struct with GC references is must-init (genCheckUseBlockInit): struct GC liveness is not reported
  precisely (TODO-1stClassStructs);
- a GC temp is not skipped like a GC-free temp (genCheckUseBlockInit), so it reaches the two reasons above;
- a GC local live into a finally is must-init (liveness MarkMustInitAndEHVars, LSRA insertZeroInitRefPositions
  for enregistered EH vars): handler-live slots are reported untracked;
- a GC spill temp is zeroed (genCheckUseBlockInit, genFnProlog, genZeroInitFrame): spill temps are reported
  untracked; a spill temp is always written before it is reloaded;
- a GC local whose first reference is an explicit init is not marked lvHasExplicitInit when a GC safe point
  precedes that init (optRemoveRedundantZeroInits).
fgVarNeedsExplicitZeroInit used to rely on the first two reasons to drop explicit zeroing of GC locals and GC
structs (newobj temps, inlinee locals with localsinit, initobj, stack-allocated objects, promotion and
tail-call-to-loop re-inits). With the knob it answers as for a GC-free local, so these inits are emitted where
semantics need them and all its consumers (importer, inliner, object allocator, promotion, redundant zero-init
removal, value numbering's initial values, async analysis) stay consistent with the prolog.

Kept: compInitMem (localsinit) zeroing, a tracked local with GC references read before its first write
(live-in to the first block; zeroed as before), and everything that does not depend on GC-ness (GS cookie,
localloc, OSR, P/Invoke frame, parameters).

Only valid when the runtime never scans a stack: a GC (or any stack walk that reads GC slots) would see
garbage in unzeroed slots.

